// Granted to this plugin's own jars, which is all a plugin policy can do -- and all it needs to do, because
// every call into the store goes through PrivilegedKeyValueStore. OpenSearch 3.x's java agent walks the stack
// and requires every protection domain on it to hold the permission, so without that privileged block these
// grants would be irrelevant: the check would fail on a server jar further up the stack, naming a jar that has
// nothing to do with this plugin. That was the first symptom, and it is worth knowing before touching this file.

grant {
  // The store backend is a jar an operator drops beside this one, and it talks to etcd, ZooKeeper or whatever
  // else over the network. Nothing in this repository names one -- D6.2 -- so nothing here can narrow the
  // permission to a known host or port either. What restricts it in practice is which backend jar is installed
  // and what URI the operator configured.
  permission java.net.SocketPermission "*", "connect,resolve";

  // ServiceLoader finds a backend on the plugin's own classloader, and gRPC and Curator both set the context
  // classloader while starting their client threads.
  permission java.lang.RuntimePermission "*", "setContextClassLoader";

  // The filesystem backend, which store-api bundles, reads and writes an ordinary directory named by
  // clusterless.store. It cannot be narrowed here for the same reason the socket permission cannot: the path is
  // configuration, and a policy file is static.
  //
  // This is the broadest grant in the file, and it is worth being clear about what it buys. The filesystem
  // backend exists so that the three planes can be run together on a laptop with no infrastructure -- it is a
  // development and demonstration convenience, not a production store, because a directory is not shared
  // between machines. A deployment that uses etcd or ZooKeeper needs the socket permission above and gets no
  // value from this one. If that becomes the only interesting case, delete this line: the plugin will keep
  // working against a real store and stop working against a directory, which is an honest trade.
  permission java.io.FilePermission "<<ALL FILES>>", "read,write,delete";
};
